.SH NAME
perldelta \- what is new for perl v5.44.0
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
This document describes differences between the 5.42.0 release and the 5.44.0
release.
.SH "Core Enhancements"
.IX Header "Core Enhancements"
.SS "Named Parameters in Signatures"
.IX Subsection "Named Parameters in Signatures"
This adds a major new ability to subroutine signatures, allowing callers
to pass parameters by name/value pairs rather than by position.
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    sub f ($x, $y, :$alpha, :$beta = undef) { ... }
\&
\&    f( 123, 456, alpha => 789 );
.Ve
.PP
Originally specified in
PPC0024 <https://github.com/Perl/PPCs/blob/main/ppcs/ppc0024-signature-named-parameters.md>.
.PP
This feature is currently considered \fBexperimental\fR, and is described in
further detail in "Signatures" in perlsub.
.ie n .SS "Multi\-variable ""foreach"" can now use aliased references"
.el .SS "Multi\-variable \f(CWforeach\fP can now use aliased references"
.IX Subsection "Multi-variable foreach can now use aliased references"
Perl version 5.22 introduced reference aliases, allowing a \f(CW\*(C`foreach\*(C'\fR loop
iteration variable to create new aliases to references. Perl version 5.36
introduced \f(CW\*(C`foreach\*(C'\fR loops with multiple variables, consuming more than one
input list item on each iteration. New in this version, the two features may
now be used together, allowing multiple iteration variables where any of them
are permitted to be reference aliases.
.PP
.Vb 2
\&    use v5.44;
\&    use feature qw( refaliasing declared_refs );
\&
\&    my %hash = (
\&        one => [1],
\&        two => [2, 2],
\&    );
\&
\&    foreach my ( $key, \e@items ) ( %hash ) {
\&        say "The $key array contains: @items";
\&    }
.Ve
.PP
Currently both the \f(CW\*(C`refaliasing\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`declared_refs\*(C'\fR features remain
\&\fBexperimental\fR.
.SS "Enhanced operation of regular expression patterns under /xx"
.IX Subsection "Enhanced operation of regular expression patterns under /xx"
Experimentally, the \f(CW\*(C`/xx\*(C'\fR pattern modifier can allow bracketed
character classes (\fIe.g.\fR, \f(CW\*(C`[a\-zA\-Z]\*(C'\fR to extend across multiple lines
and to contain comments, and to warn you of potential cases where a
portion of a pattern inadvertently has been treated as a comment instead
of what you intended.  This behavior is enabled by \f(CWuse\ feature\ "enhanced_xx"\fR.  See "/x and  /xx" in perlre.
.SS "Unicode 17.0 is supported"
.IX Subsection "Unicode 17.0 is supported"
See <https://www.unicode.org/versions/Unicode17.0.0/>.
.SS "New source of entropy for PRNG seeding"
.IX Subsection "New source of entropy for PRNG seeding"
Perl now uses the \f(CWgetentropy()\fR system call to fetch random bytes suitable
for seeding the internal PRNG. Previously Perl would read raw bytes from the
\&\fI/dev/urandom\fR device. Perl now seeds itself in this order (and falls through
upon failure):
.IP 1. 4
\&\f(CWgetentropy()\fR on systems that support this call (Linux, BSD, MacOS)
.IP 2. 4
\&\fI/dev/urandom\fR on systems that have it
.IP 3. 4
Hash of internal state variables: Unix time, process ID, and pointer value
.PP
Note that the internal PRNG is still unsuitable for security applications.
See "rand EXPR" in perlfunc for a discussion of security.
.SH Security
.IX Header "Security"
.SS "CVE\-2026\-8376 \- Buffer overflow in Perl_study_chunk"
.IX Subsection "CVE-2026-8376 - Buffer overflow in Perl_study_chunk"
Perl_study_chunk in regcomp_study.c checked the size of the joined substring
buffer in characters rather than bytes. On 32\-bit builds, this can lead to
an integer overflow of the size of the buffer leading to out\-of\-bounds writes.
.SS "CVE\-2026\-57432 \- Buffer overflow in S_measure_struct"
.IX Subsection "CVE-2026-57432 - Buffer overflow in S_measure_struct"
If you call \f(CW\*(C`pack\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`unpack\*(C'\fR to operate on a structure whose computed size
is too large to fit in memory, an integer overflow could happen that would
result in a buffer overflow. This usually happens as a result of embedding a
large number as the repeat count for an item.
.SS "CVE\-2026\-13221 \- Regex trie 16\-bit field overflow"
.IX Subsection "CVE-2026-13221 - Regex trie 16-bit field overflow"
The trie optimization in the regex engine could overflow in an alternation with more than ~65k branches. This could cause both false positives and false negatives on such regular expressions.
.SH "Incompatible Changes"
.IX Header "Incompatible Changes"
.SS "Unicode rules are now fully enforced on identifier and regular expression group names."
.IX Subsection "Unicode rules are now fully enforced on identifier and regular expression group names."
Before Unicode, Perl accepted any \f(CW\*(C`\ew\*(C'\fR character in an identifier or other
name, except the first character couldn\*(Aqt be a digit.  Later, Unicode
created two properties that described this.  Even later, they found
those properties to be insufficient, and created two new similar
properties.  These are the ones that perl has intended to use since:
\&\f(CW\*(C`\ep{XID_Start}\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`\ep{XID_Continue}\*(C'\fR.  (The \f(CW\*(C`X\*(C'\fR stands for
"eXtended" and indicates these are the more modern versions.)
.PP
(And even later, long after Perl identifier rules were formed using
the above properties, Unicode added recommendations to further restrict
legal identifier names.  These were added to counter cases where, for
example, programmers snuck code past reviewers using characters that
look like other ones.  The two properties are \f(CW\*(C`Identifier_Status\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`Identifier_Type\*(C'\fR.  See <https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr39/>.  Perl
currently doesn\*(Aqt do anything with these, except to furnish you the
ability to use them in regular expressions.)
.PP
We soon discovered that there were 14 characters that match \f(CW\*(C`XID_Start\*(C'\fR
and \f(CW\*(C`XID_Continue\*(C'\fR that don\*(Aqt also match \f(CW\*(C`\ew\*(C'\fR.  To avoid breaking code
that had long relied on \f(CW\*(C`\ew\*(C'\fR, we chose to not add those to the list of
acceptable identifier characters.
.PP
It turns out that there are about 160 characters that match \f(CW\*(C`\ew\*(C'\fR but
not the Unicode \f(CW\*(C`XID\*(C'\fR properties.  Thus they are illegal according to
Unicode.  Those are now explicitly forbidden in both Perl identifiers
and regular expression group names.  Previously, it was likely that
their use in identifiers wouldn\*(Aqt work anyway; they could be accepted
initially as legal, but other code would later reject them, but with a
message that had nothing to do with the underlying problem.  However
group names in regular expression patterns could contain illegal
continuation characters and have a higher probability of not being
caught.  That is now changed.
.PP
Only programs that do \f(CW\*(C`use utf8\*(C'\fR can be affected, and then only
characters that appear in the 2nd or later positions of the name.  The
characters that an identifier name can begin with are unchanged.
.PP
130 of the now unacceptable characters are 5 sets of 26 Latin letters
that are enclosed by some shape, such as CIRCLED LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N.
Another 8 are generic modifiers that add shapes around other characters;
5 are modifiers to Cyrillic numbers; and 16 are Arabic ligatures and
isolated forms.  The other two are GREEK YPOGEGRAMMENI and VERTICAL
TILDE.
.SH Deprecations
.IX Header "Deprecations"
.IP \(bu 4
Deprecated since 5.12, using \f(CW\*(C`goto\*(C'\fR to jump into the body of a loop or
other block construct from outside is no longer permitted.
[GH #23618 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23618>]
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`m/...[...].../xx\*(C'\fR has new restrictions
.Sp
Using an unescaped \f(CW\*(C`#\*(C'\fR or literal vertical space is now deprecated in a
regular expression bracketed character class that is compiled with the
\&\f(CW\*(C`/xx\*(C'\fR modifier.  These still work, but deprecation warnings will be
generated unless turned off or the constructs are cured as follows.
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 4
Escape a \f(CW\*(C`#\*(C'\fR by preceding it with a backslash, like in
.Sp
.Vb 1
\& m/ [ % \e# ( ) ] /xx
.Ve
.IP \(bu 4
Use constructs like \f(CW\*(C`\en\*(C'\fR instead of literal vertical space.
.RE
.RS 4
.RE
.SH "Performance Enhancements"
.IX Header "Performance Enhancements"
.IP \(bu 4
Simple (non\-overflowing) addition (\f(CW\*(C`+\*(C'\fR), subtraction (\f(CW\*(C`\-\*(C'\fR) and
multiplication (\f(CW\*(C`*\*(C'\fR) of integers are slightly sped up, as long as
sufficient underlying C compiler support is available.
.IP \(bu 4
Populating a hash from a list of key/value pairs when the keys are constant
string operands known at compile time is commonly now faster. [GH #24228 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24228>]
.IP \(bu 4
Processing of signatures at the start of a subroutine under the \f(CW\*(C`signatures\*(C'\fR
feature is now more efficient at runtime.
