.SH Documentation
.IX Header "Documentation"
.SS "Changes to Existing Documentation"
.IX Subsection "Changes to Existing Documentation"
We have attempted to update the documentation to reflect the changes
listed in this document. If you find any we have missed, open an issue
at <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues>.
.PP
Additionally, the following selected changes have been made:
.PP
\fIperlapi\fR
.IX Subsection "perlapi"
.IP \(bu 4
Auto\-generation of this document now includes the line number of the source
code, as well as its documentation.
.IP \(bu 4
It now contains information about how to find what release of
Perl first contained an API element.
.PP
\fIperlexperiment\fR
.IX Subsection "perlexperiment"
.IP \(bu 4
New entry for "New object system and \f(CW\*(C`class\*(C'\fR syntax".
.PP
\fIperlxs\fR
.IX Subsection "perlxs"
.IP \(bu 4
The reference manual for writing Perl XS code has been completely rewritten and
modernized.  It is about twice the size of the old file, and promotes more
modern XS syntax, such as ANSI signatures.
.SH Diagnostics
.IX Header "Diagnostics"
The following additions or changes have been made to diagnostic output,
including warnings and fatal error messages. For the complete list of
diagnostic messages, see perldiag.
.SS "Changes to Existing Diagnostics"
.IX Subsection "Changes to Existing Diagnostics"
.IP \(bu 4
Use of uninitialized value%s
.Sp
This warning was issued in the reverse order (right\-to\-left) when both
operands of a binary operator are uninitialized values.  This is now
fixed to be consistent with evaluation order of operands.
.IP \(bu 4
Certain diagnostics about byte sequences that are supposed to comprise a
UTF\-8 encoded character, but that are invalid in some way, now don\*(Aqt
include bytes irrelevant to that determination.  An example is
.RS 4
.IP "old message" 4
.IX Item "old message"
Malformed UTF\-8 character: \f(CW\*(C`\exc1\ex27\*(C'\fR (any UTF\-8 sequence that starts with
\&\f(CW\*(C`\exc1\*(C'\fR is overlong which can and should be represented with a different,
shorter sequence)
.IP "new message" 4
.IX Item "new message"
Malformed UTF\-8 character: \f(CW\*(C`\exc1\*(C'\fR (any UTF\-8 sequence that starts with
\&\f(CW\*(C`\exc1\*(C'\fR is overlong which can and should be represented with a different,
shorter sequence)
.RE
.RS 4
.Sp
In this case the \f(CW\*(C`\exc1\*(C'\fR is all that is needed to make the sequence
invalid.  Whatever comes after it is irrelevant (in this case, \f(CW\*(C`\ex27\*(C'\fR),
and including it in the message might lead the reader to think that it
somehow does matter.
.RE
.IP \(bu 4
The error \f(CW\*(C`Unrecognised parameters for "%s" constructor: %s\*(C'\fR has been changed
to \f(CW\*(C`Unrecognized parameters for "%s" constructor: %s\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
Variables whose name started with \f(CW\*(C`^_\*(C'\fR were incorrectly shown in
diagnostics with a literal \f(CW\*(C`Ctrl\-_\*(C'\fR (which is an invisible ASCII
character) in their name.
.Sp
The names of variables whose names begin with a caret and are longer
than two characters are now wrapped in braces, just as they have to be
in the source code.
.Sp
Therefore, using an undefined \f(CW\*(C`${^_FOO}\*(C'\fR will now correctly warn with
\&\f(CW\*(C`Use of uninitialized value ${^_FOO}\*(C'\fR, instead of the earlier \f(CW\*(C`Use of
uninitialized value $FOO\*(C'\fR (with a literal \f(CW\*(C`Ctrl\-_\*(C'\fR after the dollar
sign).
.Sp
[GH #24135 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24135>]
.IP \(bu 4
Calling the \f(CW\*(C`import\*(C'\fR method on a package with no \f(CW\*(C`import\*(C'\fR method now
produces a regular warning rather than a deprecation warning or error.
.Sp
Since Perl 5.39.1, calling \f(CW\*(C`import\*(C'\fR with arguments on a package without
such a method has triggered a deprecation warning. In Perl 5.43.6, this
deprecation was promoted into an error. This broke a significant amount
of code while providing very little advantage over the warning. This
fatal error has been converted back to a warning, with its deprecation
status removed. There are no longer any plans to make this fatal in the
future. The category for this warning is \f(CW\*(C`missing_import\*(C'\fR and it is
enabled by default.
.SS "New Diagnostics"
.IX Subsection "New Diagnostics"
\fINew Errors\fR
.IX Subsection "New Errors"
.IP \(bu 4
Can\*(Aqt redeclare catch variable as "%s"
.Sp
(F) A \f(CW\*(C`my\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`our\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`state\*(C'\fR keyword was used with the exception variable in
a \f(CW\*(C`catch\*(C'\fR block:
.Sp
.Vb 4
\&    try { ... }
\&    catch (my $e) { ... }
\&    # or catch (our $e) { ... }
\&    # or catch (state $e) { ... }
.Ve
.Sp
This is not valid syntax. \f(CW\*(C`catch\*(C'\fR takes a bare variable name, which is
automatically lexically declared.
[GH #23222 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23222>]
.IP \(bu 4
Use of "goto" to jump into a construct is no longer permitted
.Sp
(F) You have used \f(CW\*(C`goto LABEL;\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`goto EXPR;\*(C'\fR in an attempt to jump into
the body of a loop or other block construct from the outside.  As of Perl 5.44,
this throws an exception.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\ex{%X} is a \ew char that isn\*(Aqt valid in a name "%s"
.Sp
In most cases where this message now appears, an error would have
occurred anyway, but the text would not have been helpful in finding the
problem.
.PP
\fINew Warnings\fR
.IX Subsection "New Warnings"
.IP \(bu 4
Possible attempt to escape whitespace in \fBqw()\fR list
.Sp
(W qw) \fBqw()\fR lists contain items separated by whitespace; contrary to
what some might expect, backslash characters cannot be used to "protect"
whitespace from being split, but are instead treated as literal data.
.Sp
Note that this warning is \fIonly\fR emitted when the backslash is followed
by actual whitespace (that \f(CW\*(C`qw\*(C'\fR splits on).
.SH "Configuration and Compilation"
.IX Header "Configuration and Compilation"
.IP \(bu 4
C23 \fI<stdckdint.h>\fR and associated macros are now used if available.
.IP \(bu 4
It is now possible to pass to \fIConfigure\fR the values dealing with POSIX
locale categories, overriding its automatic calculation of these.  This
enables cross\-compilation to work.  The easiest way to do this is to
extract the C program that does the calculation from \fIConfigure\fR and
then run it on the target machine, and then pass the values it outputs
to \fIConfigure\fR on the other machine.  \fIPorting/Glossary\fR has examples.
[GH #22992 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/22992>]
.IP \(bu 4
The \f(CW\*(C`PERL_CREATE_GVSV\*(C'\fR configuration macro has been removed. It was almost
undocumented and it has been disabled by default since Perl 5.10.0. Its purpose
was to force all GVs to initialize their SV slot on creation. [GH #24177 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24177>]
.SH Testing
.IX Header "Testing"
Tests were added and changed to reflect the other additions and
changes in this release. Furthermore, these changes were
made:
.IP \(bu 4
Karl Williamson gave a talk at TPRC 2025 asking people to contribute
tests to find old issues that are no longer a problem, and todo tests
to reflect reproduction cases for known outstanding bugs.
(video link <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CwX4Xsf5y5E>)
It gained renewed interest this year,
leading to an uptick in test submissions from new authors,
for which we are extremely grateful.
.IP \(bu 4
When testing embedding, add a sanity check to ensure the \f(CW\*(C`libperl\*(C'\fR we
link against matches the perl we are building.  [GH #22125 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/22125>]
