.RE
.SH "Internal Changes"
.IX Header "Internal Changes"
.IP \(bu 4
The XS parser, ExtUtils::ParseXS, has been further extensively restructured
internally.  Most of these changes shouldn\*(Aqt be visible externally, but might
affect XS code which was using invalid or unsupported syntax.  Several minor
bug\-fixes were included.
.IP \(bu 4
Removed the deprecated (since 5.32) functions \f(CWsv_locking()\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`sv_unlocking\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`Perl_expected_size\*(C'\fR has been added as an experimental stub macro. The
intention is to build on this such that it can be passed a size in bytes,
then returns the interpreter\*(Aqs best informed guess of what actual usable
allocation size would be returned by the malloc implementation in use.
.Sp
This would help with sizing allocations such that SvLEN is more accurate
and not trying to shrink string buffers to save size when the intended
saving is unrealistic.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`SvPV_shrink_to_cur\*(C'\fR has been revised to include a byte for copy\-on\-write
(COW), so that the resulting string could be COWed in the future.
.Sp
It also now uses \f(CW\*(C`Perl_expected_size\*(C'\fR, compared against the current
buffer size, and does not try to do a reallocation if the requested
memory saving is unrealistic.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CWsv_vcatpvfn_flags()\fR now substitutes the Unicode REPLACEMENT CHARACTER
for malformed input.  Previously it used the NUL character.
.IP \(bu 4
For core Perl maintainers, the syntax of \fIembed.fnc\fR has been extended.
Every function, \f(CWfoo()\fR, named in that file has generated for it a
macro named \f(CW\*(C`PERL_ARGS_ASSERT_FOO\*(C'\fR.  The macro expands to \f(CWassert()\fR
calls that do basic sanity checking for each argument to \f(CWfoo()\fR that
we currently deem as being appropriate to have such checking.  (That
means that many arguments are not checked, and that the generated macro
may currently expand to nothing.)  With this release, you can add
\&\f(CWassert()\fR statements yourself to \fIembed.fnc\fR that will be
incorporated into the generated macro, beyond the system\-generated ones.
Comments and examples in \fIembed.fnc\fR give details.
.IP \(bu 4
A new function \f(CW\*(C`valid_utf8_to_uv\*(C'\fR has been added.  This is synonymous
with \f(CW\*(C`valid_utf8_to_uvchr\*(C'\fR; its reason for existence is to have
consistent spelling with the names of the other functions that translate
from UTF\-8, so you don\*(Aqt have to remember a different spelling.
.IP \(bu 4
"\f(CW\*(C`newSVsv_flags_NN\*(C'\fR" in perlapi is a new function for creating a new SV
and assigning the value(s) of an existing SV to it.
.Sp
Historically, \f(CW\*(C`Perl_newSVsv_flags\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`Perl_sv_mortalcopy_flags\*(C'\fR would
pass a new SV head and the original SV to \f(CW\*(C`Perl_sv_setsv_flags\*(C'\fR. However,
the latter contains many branches of no relevance to a fresh SV, so they
now make use of the new function to streamline the process.
.Sp
\&\f(CW\*(C`Perl_newSVsv_flags\*(C'\fR is now essentially a NULL pointer check and wrapper
around the new function, so has been moved into \fIsv_inline.h\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
"\f(CW\*(C`STATIC_ASSERT_DECL\*(C'\fR" in perlapi and \f(CW\*(C`STATIC_ASSERT_STMT\*(C'\fR are like
\&\f(CWassert()\fR, but are evaluated at compile time.  That means their
argument must be a constant expression that can be verified by the
compiler, and so they effectively have no cost, not appearing in the
code that gets executed.  These were added in v5.28, but not until now
have we opened their use up to XS writers.  At the same time, a new
variant has been added, \f(CW\*(C`STATIC_ASSERT_EXPR\*(C'\fR which is suitable for use
in an expression, such as a comma expression in a C macro.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`Perl_sv_backoff\*(C'\fR, which undoes the \f(CW\*(C`OOK\*(C'\fR string offset mechanism now only moves
the string buffer contents to the start of the buffer if \f(CWSvOK(sv)\fR is true.
Historically, the buffer contents were always moved, but if the buffer contents
are defunct and no longer required, doing that incurs an unnecessary cost
proportional to the size of the shifted contents.
.Sp
(The assumption in this change is that \f(CWSvOK(sv)\fR is a valid indicator of
whether the string buffer contents are "live" or not.)
.Sp
See [GH #23967 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23967>] for an example of
where such a copy was noticeable.
.IP \(bu 4
Fixed a bug in the "sv_numeq" in perlapi API where values with numeric
("0+") overloading but not equality or numeric comparison overloading
would always be compared as floating point values.  This could lead to
large integers being reported as equal when they weren\*(Aqt.
.IP \(bu 4
Fixed a bug in "sv_numeq" in perlapi where the \f(CW\*(C`SV_SKIP_OVERLOAD\*(C'\fR flag
would skip operator overloading, but would still honor numeric ("0+")
overloading.
.IP \(bu 4
Added "sv_numne" in perlapi, sv_numle, sv_numlt, sv_numge, sv_numgt and
"sv_numcmp" in perlapi APIs that perform numeric comparison in the same
way perl does, including overloading.  Separate APIs for each
comparison are needed to invoke their corresponding overload when
needed.  Inspired by [GH #23918 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23918>]
.Sp
This also extends the sv_numeq API to support \f(CW\*(C`SV_FORCE_OVERLOAD\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
Added the \f(CW\*(C`AMGf_force_scalar\*(C'\fR flag to the "\f(CW\*(C`amagic_call\*(C'\fR" in perlapi
API to force scalar context for overload calls.
.IP \(bu 4
Added the \f(CW\*(C`AMGf_force_overload\*(C'\fR flag to the "\f(CW\*(C`amagic_call\*(C'\fR" in perlapi
API to allow forcing overloading to be honored even in the context of
\&\f(CW\*(C`no overloading;\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
The \f(CW\*(C`SvFLAGS\*(C'\fR bits used by \f(CWSvPCS_IMPORTED()\fR and \f(CWSvOOK()\fR have now been
merged into the same position, thus freeing up a flags bit for possible future
purposes. This merge is distinguished by the fact that the \f(CW\*(C`PCS_IMPORTED\*(C'\fR
behaviour is only used on references (when \f(CW\*(C`SvROK\*(C'\fR is true), whereas the
\&\f(CW\*(C`OOK\*(C'\fR only applies to string buffers (when \f(CW\*(C`SvROK\*(C'\fR is false).
.Sp
This change shouldn\*(Aqt affect any \f(CW\*(C`XS\*(C'\fR module code that is using the test
macros correctly, though might cause confusion to code that attempts to analyse
\&\f(CW\*(C`SvFLAGS\*(C'\fR bits directly outside of the helper macros.
.IP \(bu 4
An upper limit has been applied to the multiplier operand of the
repetition operator. Constant folding will not be attempted if the
operand is a numerical constant above \f(CW\*(C`PERL_FOLD_REPEAT_LIMIT\*(C'\fR.
See [GH #20586 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/20586>] and [GH #23561 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23561>] for background.
.IP \(bu 4
Dedicated check functions \f(CW\*(C`Perl_ck_anonhash\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`Perl_ck_aassign\*(C'\fR have been
added and are used to hekify some \f(CW\*(C`OP_CONST\*(C'\fR hash keys at compile time.
[GH #24228 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24228>]
.IP \(bu 4
The macro "\f(CW\*(C`fromCTRL\*(C'\fR" in perlapi has been added to be the inverse of the
existing "\f(CW\*(C`toCTRL\*(C'\fR" in perlapi.  \f(CWtoCTRL(\*(AqA\*(Aq)\fR yields 1 (the control
character \f(CW\*(C`SOH\*(C'\fR on all platforms Perl works on); \f(CWfromCTRL(1)\fR
yields \*(AqA\*(Aq.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`Perl_check_hash_fields_and_hekify\*(C'\fR, partly called as a compile\-time
optimization, no longer preemptively converts an NV to a HEK if the
compile time and run time locales could differ.
[GH #24252 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24252>]
.IP \(bu 4
When a UTF\-8 constant string was used as the hash key in an insert
operation, such as \f(CW\*(C`$hash{\*(Aqüber maus\*(Aq} = 1;\*(C'\fR, and the string could
be converted into a single byte representation, the flag noting the
original encoding (\f(CW\*(C`HVhek_WASUTF8\*(C'\fR) was not always retained.
.Sp
When this occurred, the relevant scalars returned by \f(CW\*(C`keys %hash\*(C'\fR
would not be in the original, expected UTF\-8 encoding.
.Sp
The original encoding is now captured and propagated.
[GH #24266 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24266>]
[GH #24290 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/pull/24290>]
.IP \(bu 4
When a constant IV or NV was used as a hash key, such as in
\&\f(CW\*(C`my %hash = ( 42 => 0 );\*(C'\fR, and was preemptively stringified
into a hash key, the fact that the numerical value came first
was lost. This unintentionally modified deparse output for
such keys.
.Sp
The numerical values are now carried across, and flags on the
key variable reflect the original data type.
.Sp
Note: This now preserves the form of constant keys supplied
to tied hashes in list assignments.
[GH #24302 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24302>]
.IP \(bu 4
A new function, "defer_error" in perlapi, has been added to report
errors in compiling a perl program.  "qerror" in perlapi is a deprecated
alias for it.  Previously, \f(CW\*(C`qerror\*(C'\fR existed but was undocumented.
.Sp
[GH #23676 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23676>]
.IP \(bu 4
"op_clear" in perlapi is now part of the API.
.Sp
[GH #23676 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23676>]
.SH "Selected Bug Fixes"
.IX Header "Selected Bug Fixes"
.IP \(bu 4
Reported argument counts in \f(CW\*(C`method\*(C'\fR signatures now account for \f(CW$self\fR
.Sp
In previous versions of Perl, the exception message thrown by a \f(CW\*(C`method\*(C'\fR
subroutine with a signature when it does not receive an appropriate number of
arguments to match its declared parameters failed to account for the implied
\&\f(CW$self\fR parameter, causing the numbers in the message to be 1 fewer than
intended.
.Sp
This has now been fixed, so messages report the correct number of arguments
including the object invocant.
.IP \(bu 4
When both operands of arithmetic operators (\f(CW\*(C`+\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`\-\*(C'\fR, etc.) are
overloaded objects which have no method for that operator but have
a \f(CW\*(C`0+\*(C'\fR method and the \f(CW\*(C`fallback\*(C'\fR option set to TRUE,
perl will normally call the \f(CW\*(C`0+\*(C'\fR method for the left operand first
and then call one for the right operand, i.e. in the same order
as operand evaluation.
But if \f(CW\*(C`use\ integer;\*(C'\fR is in effect, the \f(CW\*(C`0+\*(C'\fR methods used to be
called in wrong (reverse) order.
.IP \(bu 4
Certain constructs involving a two\-variable \f(CW\*(C`for\*(C'\fR loop would crash the perl
compiler in v5.42.0:
.Sp
.Vb 3
\&    # Two\-variable for loop over a list returned from a method call:
\&    for my ($x, $y) (Some::Class\->foo()) { ... }
\&    for my ($x, $y) ($object\->foo()) { ... }
.Ve
.Sp
and
.Sp
.Vb 7
\&    # Two\-variable for loop over a list returned from a call to a
\&    # lexical(ly imported) subroutine, all inside a lexically scoped
\&    # or anonymous subroutine:
\&    my sub foo { ... }
\&    my $fn = sub {
\&        for my ($x, $y) (foo()) { ... }
\&    };
\&
\&    use builtin qw(indexed);  # lexical import!
\&    my sub bar {
\&        for my ($x, $y) (indexed(...)) { ... }
\&    }
.Ve
.Sp
These have been fixed.  [GH #23405 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23405>]
.IP \(bu 4
Several error conditions in the \f(CW\*(C`pack\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`unpack\*(C'\fR operations were
not detected and now are.
.IP \(bu 4
INTERFACE keyword in XS
.Sp
\&\f(CW\*(C`INTERFACE\*(C'\fR is now compatible with ISO/IEC 9899:2024 ("C23"), which is the
default language version used by GCC 15. Previously, it generated code that
failed to compile as C23.
.Sp
Secondly, \f(CW\*(C`INTERFACE\*(C'\fR now supports Perl package names as C types (i.e.
\&\f(CW\*(C`Foo::Bar\*(C'\fR gets auto\-converted to \f(CW\*(C`Foo_\|_Bar\*(C'\fR).
.Sp
[GH #23192 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23192>]
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`&CORE::_\|_CLASS_\|_\*(C'\fR no longer returns invalid results
.Sp
\&\f(CW\*(C`CORE::_\|_CLASS_\|_\*(C'\fR would work as expected when used as a bareword or aliased:
.Sp
.Vb 8
\&    use feature qw(class);
\&    class Foo {
\&        BEGIN { *cls = \e&CORE::_\|_CLASS_\|_; }
\&        method bar() {
\&            my $class1 = CORE::_\|_CLASS_\|_;  # ok
\&            my $class2 = cls;              # ok
\&        }
\&    }
.Ve
.Sp
But when called with an ampersand (\f(CW\*(C`&CORE::_\|_CLASS_\|_()\*(C'\fR) or through a
reference (\f(CW\*(C`my $ref = \e&CORE::_\|_CLASS_\|_; $ref\->()\*(C'\fR), it would return
unrelated strings. These runtime calls have been fixed to throw an error of the
form \f(CW\*(C`&CORE::_\|_CLASS_\|_ cannot be called directly\*(C'\fR instead of silently
returning incorrect results.
.Sp
[GH #23737 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23737>]
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CWparse_subsignature()\fR can now handle empty subroutine signatures
.Sp
Previously, calling the \f(CWparse_subsignature()\fR API function with an empty
signature would cause a "Syntax error" failure, requiring code which calls it
to detect the special case and take appropriate steps.  This is now fixed,
returning the same optree that the parser would yield for a regular empty
signature during normal parse time.
.Sp
[GH #17689 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/17689>]
.IP \(bu 4
The \f(CW\*(C`\-CA\*(C'\fR flag (or equivalently, the \f(CW\*(C`PERL_UNICODE=A\*(C'\fR environment setting)
tells perl to treat command\-line arguments as UTF\-8 strings. (See perlrun
for details.) However, this did not extend to the global variables implicitly
created by the \f(CW\*(C`\-s\*(C'\fR option:
.Sp
.Vb 3
\& $ perl \-CA \-s \-e \*(Aqprintf "%vx\en", $_ for $foo, $ARGV[0]\*(Aq \-\- \-foo=é é
\& c3.a9
\& e9
.Ve
.Sp
Here \f(CW$foo\fR would end up containing the two\-byte UTF\-8 representation of
"LATIN SMALL LETTER E WITH ACUTE", but \f(CW$ARGV[0]\fR would contain a single
codepoint corresponding to U+00E9.
.Sp
This has been fixed: If \f(CW\*(C`\-CA\*(C'\fR is in effect, options parsed by \f(CW\*(C`\-s\*(C'\fR are
treated as UTF\-8, too. In the example above, \f(CW$foo\fR and \f(CW$ARGV[0]\fR now both
contain \f(CWchr(0xE9)\fR. [GH #23377 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23377>]
.IP \(bu 4
We have long claimed to support identifiers up to about 255 characters
long.  However this was actually true only for identifiers that
consisted of only ASCII characters.  The real upper limit was as few as
64 characters for identifiers written in languages using non\-ASCII
characters, for example, Chinese or Osage.  Now an identifier in any
language may contain at least 255 characters.
.IP \(bu 4
Fixed parsing of array names starting with a digit in double\-quotish
context under \f(CW\*(C`use utf8;\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`use\ 5.42\*(C'\fR now turns on \f(CW\*(C`use\ source::encoding\ "ascii"\*(C'\fR for the
remainder of the line (besides subsequent lines). [GH #23881 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23881>]
.IP \(bu 4
Since 5.32.0, the second branch of a ternary condition operator wasn\*(Aqt
getting the correct autovivification context applied. For example in
something like
.Sp
.Vb 1
\&    @{ $cond ? $h{foo} : $h{bar} } = ...;
.Ve
.Sp
the first branch would correctly autovivify \f(CW$h{foo}\fR to an array ref,
but the second branch might incorrectly autovivify \f(CW$h{bar}\fR to a hash
ref. [GH #18669 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/18669>].
.IP \(bu 4
Don\*(Aqt warn about jumping into a construct if we\*(Aqre DIE\-ing [GH #23922 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/pull/23922>].
.IP \(bu 4
Regexes which include both a sub\-pattern (e.g. \f(CW\*(C`(??{...})\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`(?&FOO)\*(C'\fR) and
a cut (i.e. \f(CW\*(C`(?>...)\*(C'\fR) could sometimes cause a premature scope exit in
other code during a match.  For example, in something like
\&\f(CW\*(C`(?{ local $x = ... })\*(C'\fR, the \f(CW\*(C`local\*(C'\fR might have been unwound before the
pattern has finished matching.
[GH #16197 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/16197>]
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`DB::sub\*(C'\fR is not meant to be called for sub calls from the DB package, but
calls to overloaded subs for overloaded values used in the DB package would
result in calls to DB::sub.  Fixed the check for the current package in
\&\fBamagic_call()\fR.  [GH #24001 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24001>].
.IP \(bu 4
Fix parsing of hexadecimal floating\-point numbers whose significand
(aka "mantissa") values are too large to fit in UV range.
Such literals (for example, \f(CW\*(C`0x1234567890.1p+0\*(C'\fR for 32\-bit IV/UV
platform, or \f(CW\*(C`0x1234567890_1234567890.1p+0\*(C'\fR for 64\-bit IV/UV)
used to be parsed incorrectly.
.IP \(bu 4
Perl 5.42.0 did not handle the transition to/from daylight savings time
properly, fixed in 5.42.1.  The time and/or timezone could be off by an
hour in the intervals surrounding such transitions.  This was a
regression from earlier releases.  This bug was evident from perl space
in the "strftime" in POSIX function, and in XS code with any of
"my_strftime" in perlapi, "sv_strftime_ints" in perlapi, or
"sv_strftime_tm" in perlapi.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBsort()\fR optimizes common comparisons from calling the OP tree for a
comparison block into a call to a C function.  The C function used for
overloaded numeric comparisons did not handle the case where there was
no comparison overload but there was a numeric ("0+") overload
correctly, losing precision for large overloaded integer arguments that
are not exactly representable as a Perl floating point value (NV).
[GH #23956 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/23956>]
.IP \(bu 4
When a scalar variable used as the target of a filehandle is \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR\*(Aqed
while being output, garbage bytes would be left in that scalar.
[GH #24008 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24008>]
.IP \(bu 4
When a scalar variable was used as the target of a filehandle and also
was output onto itself, garbage could result, or even trigger a
segmentation fault (as in \f(CW\*(C`open $fh, \*(Aq>\*(Aq, \e$str; print $fh $str;\*(C'\fR).
[GH #24199 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24199>]
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CWclose(STDOUT)\fR when \f(CW\*(C`STDOUT\*(C'\fR has been opened as a pipe will now
properly wait for the child to exit on Windows.  [GH #4106 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/4106>]
.IP \(bu 4
Calling an XSUB via goto in scalar context, where the XSUB didn\*(Aqt return
exactly one value, could result in the return of the wrong number of
items, leading to potential stack corruption. For example:
.Sp
.Vb 2
\&    sub wrap { goto \e&an_xsub_which_returns_no_values }
\&    $ret = wrap();
.Ve
.Sp
[GH #24212 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24212>]
.IP \(bu 4
On debugging perls, instantiating a class with an array field with an empty
initializer list would abort with an error:
.Sp
.Vb 4
\&    class Foo {
\&        field @bar = ();
\&    }
\&    Foo\->new();
.Ve
.Sp
Error message: \f(CW\*(C`perl: inline.h:194: Perl_av_new_alloc: Assertion \`size > 0\*(Aq
failed.\*(C'\fR
.Sp
This has been fixed. [GH #24246 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24246>]
.IP \(bu 4
Refassigning to a class field in a method could result in the SV for
that field being released when the method exited.  The next access to
that field could result in a crash or an assertion, or if the freed SV
had been re\-used, random data.  Discussed in [GH #24187 <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/24187>]
.SH "Known Problems"
.IX Header "Known Problems"
None
.SH Obituary
.IX Header "Obituary"
"WELL VOLUNTEERED!" echoed across conference rooms and IRC channels. Matt S. Trout\*(Aqs battle
cry that transformed reluctant volunteers into community leaders.
.PP
With profound sadness, we announce Matt\*(Aqs passing. Since the early 2000s, Matt
shaped Perl through sheer force of will: IRC operator, PAUSE administrator,
Shadowcat Systems co\-founder, architect of DBIx::Class. His opinions came wrapped in
profanity and delivered at maximum volume. He suffered no fools and grew to
sometimes realize he should apologize. Yet this same abrasive exterior
protected fierce dedication to mentoring, developers he harangued into
volunteering now lead the community themselves. Matt\*(Aqs deliberately
mind\-bending code pushed Perl forward. Every modern Perl developer touches his
legacy daily.
.PP
Well volunteered, Matt. Rest in peace.
